; The format is in the kit's help\Tutorials.md: four beats per key -- say the
; key with the word it comes from, press it, hear the reader word for word,
; say what that meant. Name silence. Name no screen reader. Ten walks,
; each under five minutes, in the Homer pattern.

[feed]
Title=EdSharp Walkthroughs
Description=Spoken walks through EdSharp, the text and code editor for working by ear. Ten walks, each under five minutes, in the order a new person meets them.
Author=Jamal Mazrui
Email=jamal.mazrui@outlook.com
Base=

[about]
Title=0 - Overview
Intro=What EdSharp is, in a paragraph; the two reader keys every walk assumes; then the table of contents, one line per walk.
Setup=Nothing is needed; this walk is listened to.
Homework=Listen to the walks in order; each one assumes the ones before it.

[step]

Say=EdSharp is a text and code editor for people who listen to the screen rather than look at it. It opens plain text, Markdown, HTML and program source.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=It also opens Word documents, PDF files, slide decks and spreadsheets as text you can read and search; it compiles and runs programs, and lands the cursor on the error; it checks spelling; and it talks to an AI model on your own computer.
Key=
Hear=

[step]

Say=Everything is a key, and every key is named for a word in its command. The desktop key opens it, with an empty document ready.
Key=Alt+Control+E
Hear=EdSharp, Untitled, edit, multiline, blank
Note=Confirm the wording with a live run: buildTutorials -live.

[step]

Say=Two keys before anything else, both the reader's own. If a line goes by too fast, Insert plus Up Arrow says it again.
Key=Insert+UpArrow
Hear=(the last line, read a second time)
Say=Nothing changed; the key only repeats.

[step]

Say=And if you lose your place, Insert plus Tab says where you are: the control, its state, its position, and any hint it carries. The walks are heard with those hints off, the way most people work.
Key=Insert+Tab
Hear=(the current control, with its state and position)

[step]

Say=Now the table of contents. I say the number and the title; the reader says what the walk covers.
Key=
Hear=
[step]

Say=One, User Interface.
Key=
Hear=EdSharp's one window, the edit box you hear, dialogs that all work one way, and where help is, each named and then shown by the reader; then the rules every key follows, so a key can be guessed before it is learned.
[step]

Say=Two, Install and Launch.
Key=
Hear=The download, the installer's last page and its boxes, and EdSharp opening with Alt plus Control plus E.
[step]

Say=Three, Open, Edit and Save a File.
Key=
Hear=A file opened, a line changed, the document saved, and the two ways to hear where you are.
[step]

Say=Four, Find Your Way in a Long Document.
Key=
Hear=Finding a word, jumping to a line, and bookmarks that bring you back.
[step]

Say=Five, Convert Between Formats.
Key=
Hear=A Word document opened as text; Markdown written out as a web page.
[step]

Say=Six, Write Code.
Key=
Hear=A program compiled from the editor, the cursor landing on the error, and blocks of code moved through.
[step]

Say=Seven, Ask the AI on Your Own Computer.
Key=
Hear=A question answered and a document translated, with nothing sent off the machine.
[step]

Say=Eight, Do Math.
Key=
Hear=One want: an answer to a sum without leaving the document.
[step]

Say=Nine, Conclusion.
Key=
Hear=What to carry away, the words EdSharp uses, the help built in, and where to learn more.
[step]

Say=Ten walks, each under five minutes, under an hour together: the overview, the user interface, seven tasks, and the conclusion. They are a course, not a reference: each one assumes those before it.
Key=
Hear=
